# The third quantitative component: the one that is not about now.
#
# A meter says how much of the air is in use and a staircase says how well we hear a node. Both
# are read against a track that is right there, and neither can answer the two questions this
# client is actually opened for - is the mesh getting worse, and is that battery going to last
# the night. Those need the readings before this one, so the client keeps them (history.h) and
# this is what it does with them.
#
# Two shapes, and the scene is arranged so the difference between them is the thing on screen:
# a full-width line on a card, for a reading somebody has stopped to look at, and six cells
# against a row's trailing edge, for one the eye is passing.

scene demo
scale 4
delay 220

# ---- the row: a battery going down ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Alfa Ridge is the demo's node with a battery worth watching, and these are six telemetry
# reports of it - half an hour apart on a real mesh, which is why a node's series breaks its line
# at a silence of two hours rather than at one of fifteen minutes.
#
# The trend rides in the row's trailing slot rather than under it, because the row already has a
# picture: the banded bar on its second step is where the reading sits between flat and full, and
# that is the thing this screen is for. Six cells of line beside the figure is the direction, and
# the two together are what the percentage on its own has never been able to say - the bar is
# amber because 24% is low, and the line is why that matters.
tab nodes
battery ALFA 87
hold 260
battery ALFA 72
hold 260
battery ALFA 58
hold 260
battery ALFA 45
hold 260
battery ALFA 33
hold 260
battery ALFA 24
hold 260

# Open it and walk down to the device metrics, where the battery row is.
#
# Two downs, not one: row 0 of the Nodes tab is the Map, row 1 is our own radio and row 2 is
# Alfa Ridge. A node on mains has no battery trend to draw, so getting this wrong renders a
# screen with nothing of the component on it.
key down 2
key a
key down 26
hold 2000

# ---- the card: the mesh getting busier, and where its shape went -----------------------------
#
# `stats` and each `airtime` line after it are one LocalStats report each, which is what the
# radio sends its attached client every few minutes - so this is an hour of mesh compressed into
# a few seconds. `stats` goes first for the reason it does in gauges.scene: the counter rows want
# the radio's real traffic rather than a row of zeroes.
#
# The Status card carries the *pair* and no third row: the figure says 58%, and the bar under it
# says that is past both of its marks. Two levels of effort at one sentence, and neither replaces
# the other - a percentage has to be held against a threshold nobody carries around, where a bar
# is compared against the track it sits in.
#
# What it deliberately does not carry is the shape. There was a full-width trend line here and it
# cost this card - the most crowded on the client - two body rows to say which way the number was
# going. `trend` on the heading now opens the same readings as a chart, with the axes labelled,
# the span named, the thresholds ruled across the plot and our own transmit share beside them:
# every question the line answered and four it could not, for a verb that costs no row at all.
# See chart.scene for the press. What is left of the sparkline in this client is the six cells
# above, in a row's trailing slot - a row carries a glance, a screen carries a study.
tab status
stats
hold 500
airtime 14 3
hold 500
airtime 19 5
hold 500
airtime 26 7
hold 500
airtime 33 9
hold 500
airtime 47 14
hold 500
airtime 58 19
hold 1600
